1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,235
In 2025, Starbucks reported over

2
00:00:02,235 --> 00:00:04,020
two hundred and twenty-two million

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,091
dollars in revenue.

4
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:07,107
From gift-card money customers were

5
00:00:07,107 --> 00:00:08,535
never expected to spend.

6
00:00:08,765 --> 00:00:10,550
And here's the surprising part.

7
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:13,376
This isn't some illegal trick.

8
00:00:13,376 --> 00:00:15,303
It's a recognized accounting practice

9
00:00:15,303 --> 00:00:17,229
used by businesses that sell

10
00:00:17,229 --> 00:00:17,999
gift cards.

11
00:00:18,229 --> 00:00:20,156
Once you see how it

12
00:00:20,156 --> 00:00:22,082
works, you'll never look at

13
00:00:22,082 --> 00:00:24,008
a gift card the same

14
00:00:24,008 --> 00:00:24,779
way again.

15
00:00:25,009 --> 00:00:26,550
So here's the setup.

16
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:29,418
A man walks into Starbucks

17
00:00:29,418 --> 00:00:31,385
and buys a twenty-dollar gift

18
00:00:31,385 --> 00:00:32,959
card for his friend.

19
00:00:33,189 --> 00:00:34,369
Starbucks takes his

20
00:00:34,369 --> 00:00:35,550
twenty dollars immediately.

21
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:38,352
But in accounting terms, that

22
00:00:38,352 --> 00:00:39,873
money isn't revenue yet.

23
00:00:40,103 --> 00:00:41,244
It's a promise.

24
00:00:41,474 --> 00:00:43,376
Because Starbucks still owes someone

25
00:00:43,376 --> 00:00:45,277
twenty dollars' worth of coffee.

26
00:00:45,507 --> 00:00:47,029
It's a debt wearing

27
00:00:47,029 --> 00:00:48,550
a gift card's clothing.

28
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,407
Now, the friend visits Starbucks

29
00:00:51,407 --> 00:00:52,972
and spends fourteen dollars.

30
00:00:53,202 --> 00:00:54,376
Six dollars remain

31
00:00:54,376 --> 00:00:55,550
on the card.

32
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:57,318
Weeks pass.

33
00:00:57,548 --> 00:00:58,415
Then months.

34
00:00:58,645 --> 00:01:00,815
And the card ends up

35
00:01:00,815 --> 00:01:02,550
forgotten in a drawer.

36
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:04,940
Now multiply that by

37
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:06,058
millions of customers.

38
00:01:06,288 --> 00:01:07,778
Some spend every cent.

39
00:01:08,008 --> 00:01:09,498
Others leave a dollar

40
00:01:09,498 --> 00:01:10,615
or two behind.

41
00:01:10,845 --> 00:01:12,708
Starbucks studies historical redemption patterns

42
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:14,570
to estimate how much of

43
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,433
that money customers are unlikely

44
00:01:16,433 --> 00:01:17,550
to ever use.

45
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:19,760
And here's where

46
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:21,070
things get interesting.

47
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:23,483
Under accounting rules, Starbucks can

48
00:01:23,483 --> 00:01:25,667
gradually recognize a portion of

49
00:01:25,667 --> 00:01:27,850
those expected unused balances as

50
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:30,033
revenue, in proportion to gift-card

51
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:30,470
redemptions.

52
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:32,447
This has a name.

53
00:01:32,677 --> 00:01:33,550
Gift-card breakage.

54
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:36,029
And in fiscal 2025,

55
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:37,608
Starbucks recognized over two

56
00:01:37,608 --> 00:01:39,188
hundred and twenty-two million

57
00:01:39,188 --> 00:01:40,767
dollars in breakage revenue.

58
00:01:40,997 --> 00:01:42,971
Not one extra latte required

59
00:01:42,971 --> 00:01:44,550
for those unused balances.

60
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:47,309
So here's the strange truth.

61
00:01:47,539 --> 00:01:49,398
A company can recognize revenue

62
00:01:49,398 --> 00:01:51,256
even when the customer never

63
00:01:51,256 --> 00:01:53,115
comes back to use the

64
00:01:53,115 --> 00:01:54,231
remaining gift-card balance.

65
00:01:54,461 --> 00:01:55,948
The cash was collected

66
00:01:55,948 --> 00:01:57,435
earlier. The product may

67
00:01:57,435 --> 00:01:58,550
never be redeemed.

68
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:01,597
So next time you buy

69
00:02:01,597 --> 00:02:03,745
a gift card, ask yourself...

70
00:02:03,975 --> 00:02:06,123
Who's really receiving the gift?

71
00:02:06,353 --> 00:02:07,641
The person you're

72
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:08,930
giving it to?

73
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,877
Or the company you

74
00:02:10,877 --> 00:02:12,166
bought it from?

75
00:02:12,396 --> 00:02:14,114
Follow Oddly Logical for

76
00:02:14,114 --> 00:02:15,832
more strange business secrets

77
00:02:15,832 --> 00:02:17,550
hiding in plain sight.
